CH = dict(num=6, tag="Geography · Ch 1", theme="Types of resources, resource planning, land use and soils of India",
 title="Resources and Development",
 intro="Everything in our environment that can be used to satisfy needs, provided it is technologically accessible, economically feasible and culturally acceptable, is a resource. This chapter classifies resources, explains why careful planning and sustainable use are essential, and describes India's land use patterns, soils and soil conservation.",
 big_questions=["How are resources classified?", "Why is resource planning needed?", "How is land used in India?", "What are the major soil types and where are they found?", "How can soil be conserved?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Types of Resources"),
 ("table", ["Basis", "Types", "Examples"], [["Origin", "Biotic and abiotic", "Forests, animals, fisheries; rocks, metals"], ["Exhaustibility", "Renewable and non-renewable", "Solar and wind energy, water, forests; minerals and fossil fuels"], ["Ownership", "Individual, community, national, international", "Plots and houses; village commons, parks; all minerals, water and land within political boundaries; ocean resources beyond 200 nautical miles of the Exclusive Economic Zone"], ["Status of development", "Potential, developed, stock, reserves", "Wind and solar in Rajasthan and Gujarat (potential); resources already surveyed and in use (developed); water as hydrogen and oxygen (stock); river water used with dams (reserves)"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Sustainable development** means development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs."),
 ("h2", "Development of Resources"),
 ("ul", ["Resources were thought to be free gifts of nature, and humans used them indiscriminately. This led to depletion of resources, accumulation of resources in a few hands (dividing society into rich and poor) and global ecological crises such as global warming, ozone layer depletion, environmental pollution and land degradation.", "The **Brundtland Commission Report (1987)**, *Our Common Future*, introduced the concept of sustainable development and advocated it as a way ahead.", "The **Rio de Janeiro Earth Summit (June 1992)** in Brazil, with over 100 heads of state, adopted **Agenda 21**, a declaration to achieve sustainable development in the twenty-first century by combating environmental damage, poverty and disease through global cooperation.", "Gandhiji's words: ‘There is enough for everybody's need and not for anybody's greed.’"]),
 ("h2", "Resource Planning"),
 ("p", "Planning is essential for judicious use, especially in a country like India with diverse resources but also regional imbalance. For example, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Madhya Pradesh are rich in minerals and coal; Arunachal Pradesh has abundant water but lacks infrastructure; Rajasthan is rich in solar and wind energy but short of water; Ladakh is rich in cultural heritage but lacks water, infrastructure and minerals."),
 ("steps", [("Step 1", "Identify and inventory resources across regions through surveying, mapping and qualitative and quantitative estimation."), ("Step 2", "Evolve a planning structure with appropriate technology, skill and institutional set-up."), ("Step 3", "Match the resource development plans with overall national development plans.")]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Conservation** is not hoarding. It means using resources wisely, avoiding wastage and protecting them for the future."),
 ("h2", "Land Resources and Land Use"),
 ("p", "India has a land area of about **3.28 million sq km**, and varied relief: mountains (about 30 per cent), plains (43 per cent) and plateaus (27 per cent). Land is used for forests, land not available for cultivation, other uncultivated land (excluding fallow) and fallow lands, and net sown area."),
 ("table", ["Category", "Description"], [["Forests", "Area under tree cover; forest area is not the same as forest cover"], ["Land not available for cultivation", "Barren and wasteland; land put to non-agricultural uses (buildings, roads, factories)"], ["Other uncultivated land", "Permanent pastures, grazing lands, cultivable wasteland (left uncultivated for more than five years), land under tree crops"], ["Fallow lands", "Current fallow (left without cultivation for one or less than one agricultural year) and other than current fallow (one to five years)"], ["Net sown area", "Area sown with crops in a year; gross cropped area = net sown area plus area sown more than once"]]),
 ("ul", ["Land use is determined by physical factors (topography, climate, soil types) and human factors (population density, technological capability, culture and traditions).", "**Land degradation:** Mining in states such as Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh and Odisha leaves deep scars. Overgrazing in Gujarat and Rajasthan, over-irrigation in Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh (waterlogging, salinity and alkalinity), and mineral processing (dust) cause degradation.", "**Remedies:** afforestation, proper management of grazing, planting shelter belts, stabilisation of sand dunes with thorny bushes, control of mining activities and proper discharge of industrial wastes."]),
 ("h2", "Soil as a Resource"),
 ("p", "Soil is the most important renewable resource. It is the medium of plant growth and supports different living organisms. Its formation depends on relief, parent rock, climate, vegetation and time; soil formation is a very slow process."),
 ("table", ["Soil", "Where", "Features and crops"], [["Alluvial", "Northern plains from Punjab to Assam, Gujarat via Rajasthan corridor, east coast deltas; deposited by the Indus, Ganga, Brahmaputra", "Most widespread and fertile; rich in potash, phosphoric acid and lime; ideal for sugarcane, paddy, wheat and other crops; *Bhangar* (old, kankar nodules) and *Khadar* (new, more fertile)"], ["Black (Regur)", "Deccan Trap: Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Karnataka", "Formed from lava; clayey, retains moisture; self-ploughing; develops cracks in dry season; rich in calcium carbonate, magnesium, potash and lime but poor in phosphorus; ideal for cotton (black cotton soil)"], ["Red and yellow", "Eastern and southern Deccan Plateau; Odisha, Chhattisgarh, middle Ganga plain, Western Ghats piedmont", "Develops on crystalline igneous and metamorphic rocks in low-rainfall areas; red due to diffusion of iron, yellow when hydrated; generally poor in nitrogen and humus"], ["Laterite", "High temperature and heavy rain areas: Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Madhya Pradesh, Assam hills", "Leaching; humus low; suitable for tea, coffee, cashew with manures; used for making bricks"], ["Arid", "Rajasthan and dry areas", "Sandy, saline; low humus; moisture is low; *kankar* layer limits infiltration; with irrigation can be cultivated"], ["Forest", "Hilly and mountainous areas", "Loamy and silty in valleys; coarse in upper slopes; acidic with low humus in snow-covered areas"]]),
 ("h2", "Soil Erosion and Conservation"),
 ("ul", ["**Causes:** deforestation, overgrazing, construction and mining, running water, wind, glaciers.", "**Gully erosion:** running water cuts deep channels; **badlands** are formed (e.g. the Chambal ravines). **Sheet erosion:** water flows as a sheet over a large area and washes away the topsoil.", "**Measures:** **contour ploughing** (ploughing along contour lines), **terrace farming** (steps on slopes, as in the Himalayas and Western Ghats), **strip cropping** (strips of grass left between crops), and **shelter belts** (rows of trees planted to check wind, in coastal and dry areas)."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Define a resource.", "Anything in the environment that is technologically accessible, economically feasible and culturally acceptable to meet needs."),
  ("What is sustainable development?", "Meeting present needs without harming the needs of future generations."),
  ("What was Agenda 21?", "Declaration of the 1992 Rio Earth Summit for sustainable development in the 21st century."),
  ("Which soil is best for cotton and why?", "Black soil, because it is moisture-retentive and rich in lime and potash."),
  ("Differentiate Khadar and Bhangar.", "Khadar is newer, finer and more fertile; Bhangar is older with kankar nodules."),
  ("What is contour ploughing?", "Ploughing along contour lines to slow runoff and erosion."),
  ("Name two causes of land degradation.", "Mining and overgrazing; over-irrigation causes salinity."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Resources are classified by origin, exhaustibility, ownership and development status.", "Sustainable development was highlighted by the **Brundtland Report (1987)** and **Agenda 21 (Rio, 1992)**.", "Resource planning needs inventory, structure and matching with national plans.", "India's main soils are **alluvial, black, red and yellow, laterite, arid and forest**.", "Erosion is checked by contour ploughing, terraces, strip cropping and shelter belts."])
